requirements:
R1. Implement a vending machine in Rust as a SINGLE source file that compiles with rustc --edition 2024, std only. Output ONLY the Rust source code inside ```rust fences. Do not use inner attributes or inner documentation comments.

R2. The exact public API follows. Machine has private implementation fields. Every listed type, variant, field and method must be public. All value types listed below (everything except Machine) implement Clone, Debug, PartialEq and Eq. No other API is required.
```rust
pub struct Product { pub id: String, pub price: u16 }
pub struct State { pub credit: u16, pub selected: Option<String>, pub escrow: Vec<u16>, pub tubes: [u16; 4] }
pub struct Receipt { pub id: String, pub price: u16, pub change: Vec<u16> }
pub enum Error { EmptyCatalog, BadId { id: String }, DuplicateId { id: String }, BadPrice { id: String }, BadCoin { coin: u16 }, CreditLimit, UnknownProduct { id: String }, SameSelection, NoSelection, Underpaid { price: u16, credit: u16 }, TubeLimit { coin: u16 }, NoChange { amount: u16 } }
pub struct Machine { /* private fields */ }
impl Machine {
    pub fn new(products: Vec<Product>, tubes: [u16; 4]) -> Result<Self, Error>;
    pub fn state(&self) -> State;
    pub fn insert(&mut self, coin: u16) -> Result<(), Error>;
    pub fn select(&mut self, id: &str) -> Result<(), Error>;
    pub fn vend(&mut self) -> Result<Receipt, Error>;
    pub fn refund(&mut self) -> Result<Vec<u16>, Error>;
}
```

R3. Money, prices and coins are integer units, never floating point. The only accepted denominations are 1, 3, 4, 10. Where an array describes coin counts, indices are ALWAYS in ascending denomination order [1,3,4,10]. Credit limit is 100 inclusive.

R4. new owns its inputs; machines are independent. Each method is synchronous; all successful changes are visible in subsequent calls.

R5. new rejects an empty product vector with EmptyCatalog before any other validation.

R6. A catalog ID must consist of 1..=8 lowercase ASCII letters a..z. Any invalid ID yields BadId { id: the entire offending ID }. Runtime ID lookup is exact and case sensitive and does not run this syntax validator.

R7. new scans products in supplied order. For each product check ID syntax, then whether its ID was already seen, then price. Stop at the first failing check. DuplicateId carries the duplicate ID, even if its price is invalid. Later products never override an earlier error.

R8. Each price must be 1..=100 inclusive; invalid prices yield BadPrice { id }. Boundary prices are valid.

R9. Initially credit is zero, selected is None, escrow is empty, tubes equal the supplied counts. state returns an owned snapshot; modifying it does not affect the machine. Escrow is the accepted coin list in insertion order.

R10. insert validates denomination first (BadCoin { coin }), then whether credit + coin exceeds 100 (CreditLimit). Successful insertion appends the coin to escrow and adds it to credit. Selection does not restrict insertion.

R11. Credit 100 is permitted. A rejected insertion changes nothing, including escrow; arithmetic must not wrap for any u16 coin input.

R12. select first checks catalog membership (UnknownProduct { id: the supplied string }), then equality with the currently selected ID (SameSelection). A successful selection replaces any previous selection without changing credit or escrow.

R13. Repeated selection of the same product is an error, including when credit is zero. Any failed selection preserves the existing selection.

R14. vend checks for selection first (NoSelection). All error checks described for vend happen before any mutation.

R15. After the checks in R14, vend requires credit >= current selected price, otherwise Underpaid { price, credit }. No credit still uses this variant if a product is selected.

R16. Successful vend returns Receipt with selected ID, current price and change coins, clears selection and escrow and sets credit to zero.

R17. vend owes amount = credit - price. Change comes ONLY from tubes present before this vend; escrow is unavailable for change. Zero change always has an empty coin vector. Change selection and capacity checks are specified in R23..R27.

R18. refund succeeds even with no credit. Return every escrow coin in REVERSE insertion order, clear selection and escrow and set credit to zero. It never reads or changes tubes.

R19. All Err returns are atomic: no state, product, tube, event or audit mutation. Error payloads report the pre-call values. Result-returning methods never panic for any arguments. Successful snapshot/read methods do not mutate anything.

R20. At this level catalog IDs and prices never change, and vending has unlimited product availability. All String outputs preserve exact ID bytes. No trim or normalization occurs.

R21. Receipt and snapshot values are owned and independent; subsequent operations cannot alter earlier values. No undocumented automatic selection, refund or vending occurs.

R22. Each tube count must be 0..=100. new validates tubes only after the entire catalog passes, scanning indices from 0 to 3; first violation yields TubeLimit { coin: denomination }. Escrow coins do not count toward tube capacity until vend.

R23. First attempt GREEDY change: starting with the owed amount, visit denominations 10,4,3,1, taking min(available count, floor(remaining/coin)) at each. If this finishes at remainder zero, that exact greedy vector is mandatory even if another combination uses fewer coins.

R24. If greedy leaves a nonzero remainder, discard its tentative choices and search all exact combinations from the original tubes. Among exact combinations minimize total number of coins; among ties choose the lexicographically greatest descending coin vector (usual integer lexicographic order). If no exact combination exists return NoChange { amount }.

R25. Every change vector is sorted descending, has the exact requested value, and respects original tube availability. Greedy takes as many of a denomination as specified, including on exact boundaries.

R26. After choosing change, compute proposed tubes by removing the chosen coins from original tubes and then adding every escrow coin. If any proposed count exceeds 100, return TubeLimit for the first overflowing denomination in ascending [1,3,4,10] order. This check comes after payment and NoChange; do NOT try a different change combination to avoid overflow.

R27. On successful vend commit proposed tubes and clear escrow. insert does not deposit into tubes; refund returns escrow directly. Change deducted and escrow deposited are a single atomic update.

R28. NoChange or TubeLimit preserves selection, credit, escrow and original tubes. A caller may insert more coins, select another product or refund after such a failure.
